    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Safety</span>
    <h1>Riding a Motorbike in Hanoi Winter</h1>
    <p>Hanoi's winter is the season the brochures rarely mention. From roughly late in the year through the early months of the next, the city cools down properly: mornings arrive with mist and low double-digit temperatures, the light turns pewter-gray, and riding a motorbike changes character from the hot months entirely. Visitors who rent a bike between November and March meet a different city on two wheels, and this guide is for them.</p>
    <p>The good news is that winter riding in Hanoi is genuinely pleasant once you dress for it. The bad news is that most visitors pack for a tropical country and discover the opposite on their first morning. This page covers the season's character, the clothing solution, the way cold changes grip and visibility, and how winter habits differ from the rainy-season habits covered in our <a href="/motorbike-guides/riding-a-motorbike-in-hanoi-rainy-season/">rainy season riding guide</a>. It belongs to the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> series for renters.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
